---

<DocsLayout
  title="Release"
  subtitle="The pipeline and the updater plugin exist. Signed installers and a published feed do not."
  description="CADCAMFUN packaging, CI, auto-update, and the limits of the current release acceptance."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="status" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p>
      WP10 on the task board is <strong>done</strong> for the pipeline, CI, and the updater, with
      release secrets unset. Owner paths: <code>.github/workflows</code>, <code>scripts/</code>,
      and the updater section of <code>apps/desktop/src-tauri</code>
      (<code>updater.rs</code> and the updater block in <code>tauri.conf.json</code>). Spec:
      <code>specs/10-release-sync.md</code>.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Signed release acceptance is unverified">
      <p>
        Spec 10 says packaging scripts and workflow validation are implemented, and that signed
        cross-platform release acceptance remains unverified and blocked on owner-supplied
        signing configuration. At the recorded checkpoint, notarization, GitHub publication,
        updater signature verification against a real installer, the runtime update flow, and
        backup/rollback acceptance on a packaged app had not run. A local updater key pair can
        exist in an ignored <code>tauri-signing.json</code>. That file is not a published
        release. Apple and Windows certificates are separate and still required for
        platform-signed builds.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="pipeline" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Pipeline</h2>
    <p>
      <code>scripts/build.ts</code> is the reproducible script (Bun, not a shell script). It
      runs locally and in CI. It fails fast, prints each step, and does not read
      <code>.env*</code> files.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="text" title="Order in scripts/build.ts">
{`bun install --frozen-lockfile --os=* --cpu=*
bun run typecheck && bun test packages apps
compile sidecar -> apps/desktop/src-tauri/binaries/sidecar-<triple>
copy packages/db/drizzle/ -> src-tauri/resources/migrations/
bun run --filter @cadcamfun/desktop build
bunx tauri build
SHA-256 checksums and release notes since the last tag`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      Rust <code>x86_64</code> maps to Bun <code>x64</code>. Rust <code>aarch64</code> maps to
      Bun <code>arm64</code>. Bundles are collected only from
      <code>&lt;cargo-target-dir&gt;/&lt;triple&gt;/release/bundle</code>. The output directory
      must be empty and outside that tree. Copied names are
      <code>&lt;triple&gt;--&lt;original-basename&gt;</code>. Checksums use those copied names.
      Migration resources map <code>resources/migrations/</code> to runtime
      <code>migrations/</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Linux packages are <code>.deb</code>, <code>.rpm</code>, and <code>.AppImage</code>. macOS
      builds a separate <code>.app</code> and <code>.dmg</code> per architecture, plus the
      updater <code>.app.tar.gz</code>. There is no universal macOS binary. Windows packages are
      <code>.msi</code> and NSIS <code>.exe</code>. Modern updater assets are
      <code>.app.tar.gz</code>, <code>.AppImage</code>, and NSIS <code>.exe</code>, each with a
      sibling <code>.sig</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="ci" class="doc-section">
    <h2>CI</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Workflow</th><th>When</th><th>What</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>.github/workflows/ci.yml</code></td><td>Pull request and push</td><td>Ubuntu install, typecheck, tests, <code>cargo clippy</code>, <code>cargo test</code> for <code>src-tauri</code>. Caches Bun and Cargo.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>.github/workflows/release.yml</code></td><td>Tag <code>v*</code></td><td>Six-target matrix. Draft GitHub Release and updater manifest. Stays a draft until the owner publishes it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>.github/workflows/build-platforms.yml</code></td><td><code>build/**</code> or manual dispatch</td><td>The same six targets as downloadable artifacts, without release signing credentials.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>.github/workflows/ios.yml</code></td><td>Mobile</td><td>Expo iOS project and an unsigned simulator build. See <a href="/docs/mobile">Mobile</a>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The six targets: macOS arm64 and x64 on <code>macos-14</code> (Intel is cross-compiled),
      Windows ARM on <code>windows-11-arm</code>, Windows x64, Linux x64, and Linux ARM on
      <code>ubuntu-22.04-arm</code>. The sidecar is prepared for the explicit Rust target before
      Tauri bundles on the matching runner.
    </p>
    <p>
      Manual releases require an existing <code>v</code>-prefixed tag. The gate checks out that
      tag, installs Bun, and checks the commit and the app version. Secret-bearing builds depend
      on that gate. Branch pushes and fork release workflows are excluded. Windows imports the
      owner certificate, passes the thumbprint, and removes the certificate even on failure. No
      secret appears in an <code>if</code> expression.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>updater-manifest.ts</code> rejects duplicate platforms and requires all six platform
      keys when merging. <code>stage-release.ts</code> checks checksums, notes, and signatures,
      and stages one flat draft asset set with a combined <code>SHA256SUMS</code>. A rerun does
      not modify a published release. Draft assets are not an active updater feed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Secret names the owner sets in GitHub, and only the names belong in docs:
      <code>TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY</code>,
      <code>TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY_PASSWORD</code>, <code>APPLE_CERTIFICATE</code>,
      <code>APPLE_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD</code>, <code>APPLE_ID</code>, <code>APPLE_PASSWORD</code>,
      <code>APPLE_TEAM_ID</code>, <code>WINDOWS_CERTIFICATE</code>,
      <code>WINDOWS_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD</code>. Workflows must not echo them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Spec 10 records <code>bun test scripts</code> at 19 passing tests, a clean root
      typecheck, <code>actionlint</code> on <code>ci.yml</code> and <code>release.yml</code>, and
      a full build dry-run for <code>x86_64-apple-darwin</code> that selects
      <code>bun-darwin-x64</code> without signing credentials. That dry-run is not an installer
      you can ship.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="updater" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Updater</h2>
    <p>
      <code>tauri-plugin-updater</code> reads a static <code>latest.json</code> on GitHub
      Releases. There is no custom update server in v6.0. Signature verification is mandatory.
      The public key is committed in <code>tauri.conf.json</code>. The private key stays in CI
      secrets, or in the ignored local signing file when the owner bundles on a machine. Explicit
      environment credentials win over the local file when both are present as a pair.
    </p>
    <p>
      Behaviour specified in spec 10 §3: check at launch and every 4 hours, show a non-blocking
      prompt with release notes, forward download progress to the webview, and treat a
      <code>critical</code> flag as a prompt that cannot be dismissed until it is applied. The
      UI defers install while the editor has unsaved work. The sidecar’s migrations are
      forward-only, so every release states whether a downgrade is safe. Rollback keeps the
      previous installer link in the notes.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Rust follow-up in spec 10 bounds and coalesces check requests, runs checks serially,
      stops the task on exit, and keeps the pending announcement when installation fails so the
      user can retry. Spec 07 says updater runtime was not integration-verified during the
      lifecycle pass. <code>updater.rs</code> is present. A packaged update on a real install is
      not.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="data" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Data safety</h2>
    <p>
      Before pending migrations, WP0 copies the database with <code>VACUUM INTO</code> into the
      backups directory and keeps the last five snapshots. A backup failure aborts startup as
      <code>DatabaseError</code> with operation <code>backup</code>. Migration SQL runs in one
      transaction. Failure leaves the original logical schema. Details are on the
      <a href="/docs/database">Database</a> page. Spec 10 §4 describes the same contract from the
      release side: the shell shows the typed error, and a broken migration must not destroy the
      file.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cloud sync of rows is out of scope for v6.0. Project history syncs through the optional
      GitHub mirror in <a href="/docs/history">History</a>, not through a custom server. Soft
      delete (<code>deletedAt</code>) is not in the schema yet. Writes already go through
      repositories, which is where a later change-log hook would sit. Spec 13 says git history
      supersedes that change-log idea for project model state.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="boundaries" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Boundaries</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Scripts must not read <code>.env*</code> files or print signing secrets.</li>
      <li>The updater must not install over an unsaved editor session.</li>
      <li>Draft release assets must not be treated as the live update feed.</li>
      <li>Migration SQL must stay forward-only inside a transaction, and must not contain <code>VACUUM</code>.</li>
      <li>WP10 edits the updater configuration and <code>updater.rs</code>. It does not take over sidecar lifecycle or the database backup runner.</li>
      <li>A green <code>scripts</code> test and a dry-run are not a notarized, published release.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <DocRelated items={[{ title: "Desktop UI", href: "/docs/desktop" }, { title: "Database", href: "/docs/database" }, { title: "Sidecar", href: "/docs/sidecar" }]} />
</DocsLayout>
